    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-341407",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+8$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{3}-2(-x)^{2}+8) \\\\  & =-(-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+8) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
